  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "CInVOC9dXpXVTXYTxytBESDDeWM=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:33:22.997Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Truewind, now Axians, is an Elite OutSystems partner of 27 years that built insurance portals. On paper the profile is strong. What the research records is that receptivity collapsed on acquisition by VINCI Energies \u2014 an observation about the relationship, not about the capability.\n\nThe mechanism is worth naming because it recurs. An acquisition typically relocates the decision-making structure a partnership conversation depends on: authority over technology choices moves upward, and an initiative that used to be a local decision now competes with group priorities. The firm has not become less capable; it has become unreachable, which for this purpose has the same effect.\n\nFor the corpus this is a disqualifier with a specific and stated cause, which is materially different from a lead that simply never replied. It also flags a screening dimension: ownership changes belong in qualification rather than in background, because a strong platform record does not survive one.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 decide thesis first**\n- Truewind (now Axians) (PT)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> ACQUIRED. Elite OutSystems partner, 27 years, built insurance portals. Receptivity collapsed on acquisition by VINCI Energies.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:33:22.997650Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:33:22.997655Z"
    }
  },
